#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133716 :question/id #uuid "defe326f-f578-568c-a9b3-3d01f67da058" :question/math-academy-id "e-9558" :question/problem "![](images/64/64ba92b021de2ba2f4fc98f1ce76fa0d16b3c8b7ed9ae45237cb8772b111c8b8.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (2x^3y^2-x^5) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + (3x^4y+y^4) \\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x^3y^2-x^5$ and $Q(x,y)= 3x^4y+y^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y, \\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 12x^3y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{5})\\,dx+(3{x}^{4}y+{y}^{4})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{3}y-4{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as $\\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 1, -1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 0, 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\cdot (4-1)-(0-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =(-15)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332 :topic/difficulty 0.5673352166143972 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429099 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3627->1112. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429100 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 3 elig