ref 17592186175987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024}] :topic/math-academy-id 3627 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560}] :topic/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558972 :question-generator/id #uuid "6b09df87-9df8-5588-948a-fb2dc20165c9" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133900.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f1a9de-bb0b-570a-b792-70641d11475f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431286} {:answer/id #uuid "4724eac8-60ba-5daf-b39a-a8285610e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431305} {:answer/id #uuid "84acf7b5-dff2-5f83-9da4-34dd8d7b51ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431314} {:answer/id #uuid "9970035c-1db5-5547-b617-95eb8f16e1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431318} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc187d5-7c8b-56e2-94b6-83f788afb083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f1a9de-bb0b-570a-b792-70641d11475f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431286} :answer-field/id #uuid "b24f5ff7-bafc-59bf-b1fe-ef9b2d41540e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5478739e-f4d5-5eec-8651-b4b73e6b218c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126566" :question/problem "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeb67126-f2b3-5385-ae58-f6aa47dd1135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369394} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1f51d1-849c-5c40-98ed-fc855b6f7397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369395} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3e82f7-ed7f-5766-8b2b-b24890ff949c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369397} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba0ac0c-d2ca-5864-b0a8-1497f9993024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} :answer-field/id #uuid "83851aef-4707-5c37-b41d-06a411ef4f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23de2113-f2a7-50b0-94a3-c0894b2b5ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126645" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bda5a10df8a1ec081db97e7aa52755796f1d50be5447e3e64f55986116ada6e.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3661cf-178f-52bd-85c7-48d75d20b100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3661cf-178f-52bd-85c7-48d75d20b100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362557} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b1b3ff-21b1-5213-8b9c-584dd6a1a1eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362556}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98f26745-fe03-53ee-9421-2717c110e542" :question/math-academy-id "q-169207" :question/problem "![](images/83/8354a964dc235f1c9676754cf7d163cb4a54f05bd5899ff398acb11a4287861d.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nLet $∂R$ be the boundary of the region $R,$ shown above. Use Green's theorem to calculate the following integral:\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}^{\\phantom{C}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}={∮}_{∂R}^{\\phantom{C}}3x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a80b35-ba1d-5225-b811-45d42e3518c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369506} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d29cd2-757a-5f31-a966-786ef1201ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{242\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369507} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7bf771-68a8-5635-b443-270be3859a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369508} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1b880d-8ca7-5039-a151-5e9c415014b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "728\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369509} {:answer/id #uuid "db63a2b7-a407-5f7d-b9b2-4abcfc16c974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7bf771-68a8-5635-b443-270be3859a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369508} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe2c0c3-ccf0-5837-bf7d-eba354f732ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369505}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a38e1386-54d8-53c8-80be-ec42c2b70086" :question/math-academy-id "q-126935" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{4}y-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot ({3}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{728\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb00ccb-3eb2-54ed-b39e-a1b797a275e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369513} {:answer/id #uuid "7353f729-db5d-5849-9168-5e4b921191db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369514} {:answer/id #uuid "f2684cd6-1f99-5ede-94b6-e3ceba6e28d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-145\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369515} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d8e429-0275-503d-8cb9-59c096c6a40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369516} {:answer/id #uuid "1b918db5-2ba3-5ac0-8eae-fc4b0f13cbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d8e429-0275-503d-8cb9-59c096c6a40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369516} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d326558-bc4e-5ef3-84ac-5f28a471d7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88b73727-8c06-5f8a-b431-35e41d39a38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126937" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6252037001e1a5548ede86a16b4ddde8f5aec8fd956bded55a3506f7b533dbe.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n{∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\operatorname{ln}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\operatorname{ln}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2\\le r\\le 4,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({4}^{4}-{2}^{4}) \\\\  & =-120\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d05767d-a65e-5d43-a488-57f4105f6090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369520} {:answer/id #uuid "b9437141-8677-5d36-ade8-56553767eb3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369521} {:answer/id #uuid "71c53a26-6b18-5e5a-a6b4-14d20c2a1939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369522} {:answer/id #uuid "c90c6307-93a1-5a42-b489-784db15ed739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369523} {:answer/id #uuid "d963956c-f4e3-525a-b6b9-91fd86eb5922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d05767d-a65e-5d43-a488-57f4105f6090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369520} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c1ef24-72d4-585c-9c7b-034e01ca1112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369519}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae4fa615-3a59-509f-96cf-981693bf3db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126941" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b232a4cddf05a425709c1b3204b69b0126e586b42a6af73b867dd75ee294d961.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{x}^{3}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${\\mathrm{Δ}}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${\\mathrm{Δ}}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006e746d-7483-59b3-ba9c-8cd280691f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369527} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc0003-b805-567e-8d14-5ab416216a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369528} {:answer/id #uuid "14561ae1-e3be-5f9d-bccd-fde3e73d6059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369529} {:answer/id #uuid "da7adf00-3485-5b42-ac23-7efeb71d22ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369530} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531} :answer-field/id #uuid "1303c02b-a3ee-52e9-98a2-763dbec18e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369526}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904872cd-e02c-55d9-9792-17de482b0907" :question/math-academy-id "q-126945" :question/problem "![](images/75/754aa1ded10b20362350f3da9e5b4dbe5f9f194c2289af348ff9de34f2fb8cc5.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (2-\\frac{1}{2})-(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247e7c9b-86e2-5f51-8ba9-c9448bc0dc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431290} {:answer/id #uuid "408f7865-0008-58e6-85c6-ac75d60946d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431301} {:answer/id #uuid "4618a806-c076-56af-a407-64d1881a0e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431302} {:answer/id #uuid "51464593-b024-55b5-8ac6-2bf2b769ffdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431306} {:answer/id #uuid "c459028d-7c56-50dd-8fbf-e3321e5298d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247e7c9b-86e2-5f51-8ba9-c9448bc0dc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431290} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a97b187-3262-5c06-b7ed-fed9c7d6dc5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "420adaa9-fbd3-5686-bca6-a55b93dae680" :question/math-academy-id "q-126939" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1a063fd261e790a8c3a56f741d0356f963300c3fbd88ddde03aa1744ef81053.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{4}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3}).$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}-(-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot ((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{5}}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236daf0a-8fec-5986-adef-4fec2089883e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431289} {:answer/id #uuid "2980d347-4cd0-508a-a19e-609126e74ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431291} {:answer/id #uuid "b95f6d7e-5d5f-5174-809f-affea38f8fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431328} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d3bde0-ed52-52bf-9585-a17e40e4ed16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431329} {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b281059-95fd-5ff3-8dbf-e28f1dfbb9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431341}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93158d64-29fd-580d-9bf5-f56c5e2adb43" :question/math-academy-id "q-126938" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f39220d-1633-509c-81a8-5131d70771c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431293} {:answer/id #uuid "39f62df8-11d8-553d-8fdd-204067835d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431296} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c4c9a-4ad0-5f0f-96f5-e727f1d26528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431299} {:answer/id #uuid "59acde88-fd8f-54be-a537-e285b05463de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431307} {:answer/id #uuid "5bef94c4-e6c8-5f82-b55a-2a267716a18a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c4c9a-4ad0-5f0f-96f5-e727f1d26528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431299} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21d5399-1389-5996-9269-7e769b0bb606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d01c58ab-6639-5fe1-a3d3-01aeb78816a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126942" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbe6009d42623d3b3cd9f05380159bb0f4b8f22b920c1ef5d4d82b5dbf152d4a.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y(x+1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6xy+2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6xy+2y)-2y(x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(9-0)\\cdot (4-1)-(4-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d37215b-1943-5905-97aa-565540294dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431300} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b7de02-99e4-5e8d-9b2d-adc7d46bc083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431320} {:answer/id #uuid "a55fde2d-708a-592b-8240-a4b285208e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431321} {:answer/id #uuid "adc8174a-05b0-5d22-bae6-7c69965d1aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431322} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1df00-1a0c-583b-9b5b-65ba53909dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1df00-1a0c-583b-9b5b-65ba53909dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e0020cb-0a2c-5126-8edf-5125f13da402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431343}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f20c5355-cea7-5f07-9f8f-ed2bfe376f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-126946" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9be396fd57559bb77f92d5a0421f3a7cdca577203904e577da0caf5326caac91.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x$ and $Q(x,y)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-8{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{2}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))\\cdot (16-16)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc93cb5-dc56-5ce8-9e29-5d04cbab18f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441593} {:answer/id #uuid "590d73f3-e82d-557d-94b6-037ec0668422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,880" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441594} {:answer/id #uuid "63245646-a801-5cdf-a8ba-6eca492c0f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} {:answer/id #uuid "753d4b97-5af5-5ac8-b2eb-b98600ccb71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441598} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e4fff1-a46e-50d1-90e7-531220bb1a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63245646-a801-5cdf-a8ba-6eca492c0f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e8d331-dfbd-5435-859b-6678685b6ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441614}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e02e690-e43f-5ecb-8751-7e2242b3bc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-126936" :question/problem "![](images/23/23d35daa15e11899592683f2f3215506cc0337358be843780dbf1190c0e38e2c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 8,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{4}}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{4}^{8} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bc592f-463c-5b10-94de-5b44d176d00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e63af1d-9e8f-51ce-b89b-69668a98103f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441591} {:answer/id #uuid "6f81656c-ec2b-52fd-99b0-096af9bbcd54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441597} {:answer/id #uuid "e220a614-c017-51de-896b-5544add5e4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441606} {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608} :answer-field/id #uuid "25134893-42bb-5ae1-bdf7-77b092bdf96e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441611}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "311f57fc-3b6c-595d-a8b9-91e8d4d5d656" :question/math-academy-id "q-126947" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ab320e0f1426b846c7bf561b977ac6458c80d235aabdc5647a7db07c4cb74.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ and $Q(x,y)=5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=10x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}10x{y}^{2}-6x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(8-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-(2-0)\\cdot (0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b470497-90e8-56a7-b5fd-525d6c5ea5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 609 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558942} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4d5100-ec2b-5e19-978c-eb1f7f60b5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "609 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558946} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a472c-0ddd-5258-a8ab-790f0023bd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 156 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558948} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eade2b-d071-5bf1-874a-759e98bba81c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 641 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558960} {:answer/id #uuid "e29f1371-1749-5228-8459-43c11b397e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 625 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b470497-90e8-56a7-b5fd-525d6c5ea5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 609 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558942} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdb48500-ed1a-5001-8de4-1c2f4192c5e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558971}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19907c37-a4a5-570e-a985-43945896ae6b" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e863fbe1fbcc931a1acaf67fe92990d4bd0551f8c5a1a89a01c8eeba367d668.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):2\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{2}^{5}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(4\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((2)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(16\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 609 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af40678-d633-58fd-8023-0baebfff7a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558930} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa7ad34-5512-5cc7-9fd4-05fd770e6d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 175 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558933} {:answer/id #uuid "39c2f528-d5a0-5329-a22c-aa36fac10c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 256 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558937} {:answer/id #uuid "99b54d11-c830-598b-b0e1-cb4eb98e9745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 337 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558952} {:answer/id #uuid "b09e3af2-5813-5016-bf1a-7d98fb4fbdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{148 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa7ad34-5512-5cc7-9fd4-05fd770e6d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 175 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558933} :answer-field/id #uuid "218de214-22a2-5d9c-9748-a930f5df5fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b6c6718-cecd-5d44-9775-b44c08c7c182" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{4}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 175 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/re