ed}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{1}^{5}2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{1}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{1}^{5}\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((1)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\pi\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =624 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4020541d-b158-5694-b381-6ba67f8e0fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{196 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558938} {:answer/id #uuid "4757fc24-3b66-50f2-9608-ded9a4e2810b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "272 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558939} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f6f4b4-0951-5767-8e49-9e2c75c41a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "353 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558954} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d2213f-eb4a-56a0-b993-71fb6d52fdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558958} {:answer/id #uuid "c15608a7-e710-593f-9bfd-3bc890df6bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 272 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4757fc24-3b66-50f2-9608-ded9a4e2810b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "272 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558939} :answer-field/id #uuid "596aed75-fbff-545e-94b0-567c1e8c389c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558940}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0e8fd21-3500-5007-9a3c-aa39f399cf8f" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b73bdd66e1e9ac758c98d854bfe93b6fcf82ff8d85322f607e50b75a5797770f.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{5}\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =272 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3049bbac-81f9-5220-9af1-9aa47b7cf658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 350 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558935} {:answer/id #uuid "a431815c-7a85-5b9c-8b60-bcccf96dc0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "350 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558955} {:answer/id #uuid "c50710fd-1e48-5ef7-a0b4-bd5e5698ad57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{296 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558964} {:answer/id #uuid "efcdabfc-a8f4-5979-8d11-663f01462fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 674 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558969} {:answer/id #uuid "fac8ee63-1e41-5a4a-a535-7bac2932e547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 512 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3049bbac-81f9-5220-9af1-9aa47b7cf658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 350 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558935} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a78c00f-da46-5e8a-8243-86c98db8401d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e873ebd8-54e2-5508-997d-3107e1ab89bf" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x^{2} y + y^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=- x^{3} - x y^{2}.$ So,\n\nFor $\\partial P/\\partial y$ we hold $x$ fixed, and for $\\partial Q/\\partial x$ we hold $y$ fixed. The component derivatives, including zero contributions, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} y + y^{3}\\right)=\\left(3 y^{2}\\right)+\\left(x^{2}\\right)=x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{3} - x y^{2}\\right)=\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)+\\left(- y^{2}\\right)=- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + 3 y^{2},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}.$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D\\left(- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}-(x^{2} + 3 y^{2})\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D - 4 \\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 4 \\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{4}\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =-1\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 2 \\pi\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 350 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}]}
